      "type": "text",
      "text": "reduced by one standard deviation. This method ensures\r\nthat the durability estimate reflects a lower-bound\r\nconfidence level, mitigating the risk of overestimating long￾term carbon storage.\r\nAs such, is calculated as:\r\n(Equation 6)\r\nWhere:\r\nis the fraction of inert carboniferous material\r\nin a sample after 1000 years.\r\nis the mean of R₀ measurements, in %.\r\nis the individual measurements of , in %.\r\nis the number of measurements for .\r\nis the mean of measurements,\r\nin %.\r\nis the individual measurements of\r\n, in %.\r\nis the number of measurements for .\r\nQuantification of biochar storage for Crediting in the built\r\nenvironment will be updated in future versions of this Module\r\nin line with the best available science as more research in\r\npeer-reviewed literature is published.\r\nFDurable\r\nFinertinite = R0 − R − ×\r\nnR − 1 0\r\n1\r\ni=1\r\n∑\r\nnR0\r\n( 0,i R0)\r\n2 (Cnon-reactive −\r\nFinertinite\r\nR0\r\nR0,i R0\r\nnR0 R0\r\nCnon−reactive Cnon−reactive\r\nCnon−reactive,i\r\nCnon−reactive\r\nnC Cnon−reactive\r\n7.2.1.3.2\r\nCalculation of FReversal\r\n03/02/2026, 10:56 Biochar Storage in the Built Environment v1.0 — Isometric\r\nhttps://registry.isometric.com/module/biochar-storage-built-environment/1.0 35/70"
