      "type": "text",
      "text": "This Module distinguishes between for biochar\r\nstored in concrete and biochar stored in asphalt. Note that\r\nfor biochar stored in asphalt is assumed to be\r\n100%, and thus the labile fraction is not included in\r\ncalculation of .\r\nin concrete is determined through a geochemical\r\nmodel that assesses the weathering of a given built material\r\nover 1,000 years. An overview of the geochemical model\r\nused for this assessment and key assumptions underlying\r\nthis model are given in Section 10.0.\r\nThe default value, based on a worst-case assessment\r\n(described in Section 10.0), is 2%. Project Proponents may\r\nchoose to submit additional documentation to conduct a\r\nproject-specific reversal assessment. As shown in Equation\r\n5, a safety factor of 3 is applied to to ensure a\r\nconservative estimate. This approach, and the safety factor\r\napplied, will be reassessed as scientific consensus evolves.\r\nFReversal\r\nFReversal\r\nFDurable,Material\r\nFReversal\r\nFReversal\r\nis defined as the portion of biochar that is not\r\nconsidered inertinite (e.g. does not pass the 2%\r\nthreshold). It is calculated as:\r\n(Equation 7)\r\nWhere:\r\n7.2.1.3.3\r\nCalculation of FLabile\r\nFLabile\r\nR0\r\nFLabile =\r\nmBiochar,total\r\nmBiochar,total − mBiochar,inertinite\r\n03/02/2026, 10:56 Biochar Storage in the Built Environment v1.0 — Isometric\r\nhttps://registry.isometric.com/module/biochar-storage-built-environment/1.0 36/70"
